### Comprehensive Architectural Study: AWT vs. Java Swing

#### 1. Why Swing Replaced AWT:
The original Abstract Window Toolkit (AWT, JDK 1.0) relied on native operating system windowing peers. This created severe limitations:
- **Heavyweight Nature:** Every AWT component occupies an opaque native OS window handle, consuming excessive OS resources.
- **Inconsistent Geometry:** Differences between native platform UI engines caused components to clip or misalign across operating systems.
- **Least Common Denominator:** AWT could only provide components present in *all* supported operating systems.

Swing (introduced in Java Foundation Classes, JFC) solved these limitations by painting all components directly onto the screen buffer in 100% pure Java without native peer handles.

#### 2. Detailed Technical Comparison Table:
| Comparison Parameter | Abstract Window Toolkit (AWT) | Swing Framework (JFC) |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Package** | `java.awt.*` | `javax.swing.*` |
| **Native Dependency** | Heavyweight (Bound to OS C/C++ peer window). | Lightweight (No native peers; painted in Java). |
| **Look and Feel** | Strictly determined by host OS theme. | Pluggable Look and Feel (Metal, Nimbus, Windows). |
| **Transparency & Shapes** | Strictly rectangular, opaque. | Supports translucent components, non-rectangular shapes. |
| **Memory Footprint** | Low initial load, but leaks native OS handles. | Slightly higher RAM load, but deterministic JVM GC. |
| **Component Naming** | `Button`, `Frame`, `List`, `Menu` | `JButton`, `JFrame`, `JList`, `JMenu` |

#### 3. Complete Demonstrative Java Implementation:
```java
import javax.swing.*;
import java.awt.*;

public class AwtVsSwingDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Swing GUI execution should occur on the Event Dispatch Thread (EDT)
        SwingUtilities.invokeLater(() -> {
            JFrame frame = new JFrame("Swing Lightweight Component Demonstration");
            frame.setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE);
            frame.setSize(450, 250);
            frame.setLayout(new FlowLayout());

            // Swing Components prefixed with 'J'
            JLabel label = new JLabel("Pure Java Lightweight Swing Components:");
            JButton button = new JButton("Interactive Button");
            JCheckBox checkBox = new JCheckBox("Pluggable Look & Feel Enabled", true);
            JProgressBar progressBar = new JProgressBar(0, 100);
            progressBar.setValue(75);
            progressBar.setStringPainted(true);

            // Add action listener
            button.addActionListener(e -> {
                JOptionPane.showMessageDialog(frame, "Swing components paint smoothly without native OS peer lag!");
            });

            frame.add(label);
            frame.add(button);
            frame.add(checkBox);
            frame.add(progressBar);

            frame.setLocationRelativeTo(null); // Center on screen
            frame.setVisible(true);
        });
    }
}
```

$$\therefore \text{Swing eliminates native platform lock-in, enabling portable, uniform Java GUI applications.}$$
